route: visual

---

[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'You are only six steps away from creating a fully realistic AI avatar and none of them require you to film anything or hire anyone. The problem with every avatar workflow right now is that the face changes between videos and the voice doesn\'t stay consistent with'

[00:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the character. So, it [music] never feels like an actual person. But in this video, I\'ll take you through all six steps. And by the end, you\'ll have your own avatar that looks and sounds like the [music] same person in everything you make. So, let\'s start with the image'

[00:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that will decide whether people believe our character is a real person or not. Everything in this video happens inside Higsfield, which is an all-in-one AI platform with the top image, video, and audio models in one place, and I\'ll'

[00:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'leave a link to it in the description below. I\'ll also give away all the prompts I\'m using for free. So, to begin with, I\'ll click image at the top navigation bar inside Higsfield and click create [music] image. Next, I\'ll'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'set the model to Nano Banana Pro. Then I\'ll set the aspect ratio to 16x9 because this image will be a wide one with three panels inside it. And I\'ll push the quality up to 4K because this one frame is what every single'

[01:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'generation after it pulls from. So the sharper and higher quality this is, the better the outputs will be later. I\'ll paste in the prompt for our character sheet and hit generate. In this image, we have three angles of our character. The first and second one showcase her'

[01:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'clothing from the front and behind plus her hair. And next, we have a close-up shot of our character. If you notice, there are pores, little black spots, and some patchiness in her eyebrow and a mole below her eye. She also has a pair'

[01:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of earrings, which are very unique, and we can see her individual hair strands separately in a messy way. These imperfections make our character feel more real and better than a cleaner, polished look. Now, if you had just'

[01:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'asked for an attractive woman in her mid20s, you would get something that looks way too perfect, and it\'s not something we want. Another thing worth pointing out is that the light is intentionally flat and direct because we don\'t really want our AI model to take'

[01:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'reference from any distorted or funky lighting. Next, to make this character sheet reusable in any generation we want, I\'ll save her as an element inside Higsfield. [music] That way, if I reference her with an at'

[02:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'symbol in any prompt box, it pulls directly from this image. I\'ll set her category to character and hit create. So, if you build a character sheet like this once, you can reuse it in any prompt box from then on. But everything'

[02:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you\'ve seen so far is one still image under a perfect studio light. And nothing about that is hard for a model to keep consistent. So, let\'s see how our character behaves once we put her in an actual [music] video. First, I want'

[02:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'her to review a product. So, for that reason, I\'ll change the image generation model to GPT image 2 because it\'s better at creating product images and set the aspect ratio to 1x one. Next, I\'ll paste in this prompt and hit generate. We got'

[02:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a really simple looking protein bar with big text on the wrapper. And I\'ll save this as an element the exact same way I did for our character, but this time for the category, I\'ll select prop instead of character. Next, I\'ll click video at'

[02:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the top and go into create video. The model is already set to seance 2.0. By the time you\'re watching this, seance 2.5 will already be out and available on Higsfield, so feel free to use that.'

[03:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'ll bump the duration up to 15 seconds and set the aspect ratio to 9x6. I\'ll leave the resolution on 1080p because generating at native 4K requires a lot of credits and it wouldn\'t be'

[03:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sustainable for any kind of normal workflow. At the end, I\'ll upscale the videos to 4K anyway, so doing this saves us a lot of credits. Now, I\'ll attach our references. So, I\'ll click at elements under the prompt box and pull'

[03:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in both our female character and the product. The audio toggle will stay on because she\'s going to be talking in this one. and I\'ll drop in the prompt and hit generate. Okay, this is the power bar everyone kept telling me about.'

[03:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It\'s actually good. Not chalky, doesn\'t taste like cardboard, and it keeps me full till lunch. [gasps] Anyway, back to work. I just wanted to show you this one. In the video, we can see our character talking about our'

[03:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'product, describing what it tastes like, and saying it\'s not like most protein bars. She\'s in a room inside her apartment recording with the selfie camera on her phone. If you notice the way she speaks, it has a very similar tone and rhythm to how a real person'

[04:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'speaks because it\'s not so polished that it starts sounding robotic. Though, it\'s not 100% natural. And if we take a look at the prompt we put in, everything inside it is pushed towards making the video look worse. I also told it there\'s'

[04:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cool daylight coming from a window on one side and warmer [music] kitchen light behind her. So, the two colors don\'t match and it comes out looking uneven. The reason I did that is the same as before. to stop our video from looking polished because anything made'

[04:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to look super perfect with no flaws at all is generally the biggest reason people are able to catch that it\'s AI generated and her dialogue is also written word for word because if we didn\'t do that the model would have made'

[04:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'up its own dialogue and it wouldn\'t have sounded good at all. So, the lesson is when you\'re generating videos and you want them to look as realistic as possible, include the flaws and imperfections on purpose. Because'

[05:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] when someone actually films something in real life, the conditions are never perfect. And that is exactly what we\'re recreating. But this is one room with the light barely changing and she hasn\'t moved from her spot once. So,'

[05:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'let\'s see if our character can pull off a proper travel vlog. And for that, I need the place she\'s going to walk through. So, I\'ll go back into image and set the model to Higsfield Soul 2.0. Next, I\'ll change the aspect ratio to'

[05:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '16x9. And I\'ll paste in the prompt for our location. This looks like a prime location set near a luxury resort a little way back from the shore. And this place has a mix of different things going on like the concrete safety wall'

[05:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'here and the old school looking pillars supporting the wooden top part. And the floor also looks really old and rugged. The one thing I did differently here is I told the model to generate the place completely empty of people because this'

[05:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'isn\'t really meant to be a photo. It\'s going to be a reference and anyone standing inside it will become something the model regenerates every time it makes a new video. So I\'ll also save this to elements and I\'ll select the category as location. Now the location'

[06:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'element controls the geography, the materials, the light direction and the atmosphere [music] of a place. But what it doesn\'t control is the camera angle or the framing. So all of that will be controlled by what we write inside the'

[06:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'prompt. So I\'ll head over to the video tab and keep all the settings the same as the last one. The only thing I\'ll change is the aspect ratio to 16x9 because this will be framed wide instead of vertical. I\'ll pull in our character'

[06:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and the terrace from elements. Drop in the prompt and hit generate. We\'ve been walking like 20 minutes and every street looks like this. [laughter] Wait, come here. Look at this. the tiles, the river down there. Yeah,'

[06:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'m not leaving this place. [sighs] In this video, she seems to be walking down an alleyway while recording herself. And honestly, the location is not really good-looking in particular,'

[06:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but as she moves and reveals, we can see she\'s come into the reference image we created a little earlier, and our character also said how she really likes the place. The camera doesn\'t have that much stabilization [music]'

[07:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and has a lot of shakiness and random movements, which helps simulate the conditions of a real vlog. If we take a look at our prompt, it\'s broken up into instructions for the specific elements in the video so that we can control each thing and tune it to our needs. As you'

[07:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can see, there are 11 separate blocks in there, and most of them do exactly what the name says. [music] The one worth pointing out is positive locks at the bottom, which is a list of things that must not change no matter what the model'

[07:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'does with everything else. And if you want even more control, you can add blocks that aren\'t in here, like a color palette block for how the colors come out or optics for the lens you want. So add a block for every part of the video'

[07:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you actually care about, because anything you leave out is something the model decides for you, and it\'s not something we always want. Now, what if we want to do something [music] completely different? So, let\'s find out how far we can push our character before'

[07:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'she stops looking like the same person. The last two videos were both meant to look cheap and unplanned, filmed on a phone in one unbroken take. This video is going to be the exact opposite, which is a proper cinematic sequence with a'

[08:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'locked camera including real shots and cuts between them. And that is the hardest test by a long way because [music] getting something to look realistic and cinematic at the same time is a lot for the AI to pull [music] off.'

[08:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now, for this one, our location is a cold place, so we need to dress our character in a proper cold weather outfit. So, I\'ll head back into image with Nano Banana Pro as the model. Keep everything the same and paste in this'

[08:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'prompt with our female character attached as a reference. [music] As you can see, she has a puffer jacket and a pair of rugged shoes with thick wool socks alongside nylon pants. Though, the shirt she was wearing previously is the'

[08:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'same. And now, this will become our new reference sheet. I\'ll save this one to Elements as well. And now I\'ve got the same person in two different sets of clothes that I can call up whenever I want. Now for the video, I\'ll head back to the video tab, keep the model and'

[09:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'duration where they were, and set the aspect ratio to 21x9. That\'s the really wide letter box shape you get in films, and Seance generates it natively, so there\'s no need for cropping or refframing afterwards. The audio toggle'

[09:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'will stay on this time, too. And I\'ll attach the new element, paste in the prompt, and hit generate. The video starts with a drone zoomin'

[09:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'shot over a mountain with a lot of clouds beneath it and it looks like the sun is about to rise over the far horizon and it has a very cool bluish tone which is perfect for the environment we chose. In the next cut, we see our female character coming out'

[09:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of her tent with a mug in her hand. Then the camera changes to a close-up shot as she takes a sip of her morning drink. And in the last one, we can see a zoom out drone shot instead. All of these elements combined made our video look'

[10:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cinematic and realistic at [music] the same time with our character and location being consistent throughout multiple shots. If you take a look at our prompt, I\'ve included an optics block, and I broke the video down into the different cuts with detailed'

[10:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'instructions for what I want in each one. Inside that block, I set every shot with a field of view number in degrees, which is just how much of the world the lens takes [music] in at once. So, that opening drone shot is 107°, which is why'

[10:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the whole mountain range and the clouds underneath fit [music] in. The one where she comes out of the tent is 84°, and the close-up while she\'s drinking is 18, which takes in almost nothing except'

[10:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'her. And that\'s the shot where her face has to hold up the most. So work out how much of the world you want in your frame and the number comes from that because the bigger the number, the wider your frame will be. And because we didn\'t generate the place beforehand, I had a'

[10:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'location map block in the prompt for where I wanted the video to be. We also had a really soft ambient sound playing as the video played. And that came from including an audio block in the prompt describing the exact type of song I'

[11:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wanted. So whenever you need her in different clothes, regenerate the sheet instead of the character and you can keep the exact same face every time. But going back to our first two videos, even though her voice had a good rhythm and'

[11:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tone to it, as I mentioned, she didn\'t sound 100% natural. So let\'s find out if you can describe a voice the same way we described the videos and the images. I\'ll click audio at the top of the'

[11:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'navigation bar. And in this tab, there\'s a menu on the left with three modes: text to speech, voice change, and translate. I\'ll go with text to speech and I\'ll switch the model over to seed audio 1.0. The whole settings row is'

[11:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'fine as it is and I\'m not selecting anything from choose [music] voice. Then I\'ll paste in the prompt and hit generate. I moved here about 2 years ago and honestly I still get lost walking to the same coffee place. There\'s a bakery'

[11:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on the corner that opens at 6:00 and I\'ve never once made it there before 10:00. Last winter it rained for three straight weeks and honestly it sounds much better than the original audio'

[12:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cedence generated itself. It\'s much more crisp and sounds more natural than the one we heard before. What I pasted in the prompt wasn\'t really a script. It\'s a description of a person followed by the exact dialogue I wanted her to say.'

[12:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I described a woman in her mid20s with a light warm tamber and a slight rasp and a bit of vocal fry at the end of her phrases and seed audio built the voice out of that. I also wrote the delivery'

[12:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'as bright and animated rather than flat because whatever energy is in this sample carries into every video you use the voice on later. So to generate voices like these, describe the exact details and nuances of how you want the'

[12:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'whole thing to sound. And the results you get will be much better than any generic voice tool with plainly [music] pasted scripts out there. But this is just an audio file sitting in my library. And our character in those'

[12:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'videos still has whatever voice Cance gave her. So let\'s fix that. First I\'ll click voice change. [music] That opens to two panels. Pick a voice and add your clip. So I\'ll click pick a voice. Upload the audio sample I just made and I\'ll'

[13:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'give it a name. After that, it\'ll appear as a [music] custom preset in the voice preset list alongside the stock ones. That will cost approximately 40 credits and take about a minute. The reason you'

[13:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'have to do this at all is that seed audio only gives you an audio file, and an audio file can\'t be attached to anything. Cloning is what turns it into a preset you can actually select. Next, I\'ll click add your clip, which turns'

[13:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'into a reference video slot. So, I\'ll put the kitchen video straight in it with Ava as the voice selected and hit generate. Okay, this is the power bar everyone kept telling me about. It\'s actually good. Not chalky, doesn\'t'

[13:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'taste like cardboard, and it keeps me full till lunch. Anyway, back to work. I just wanted to show you this one. As you saw, this voice sounds much better with our character. And honestly, if you saw this'

[13:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the first time without prior context, you wouldn\'t have been able to tell whether this was an AI generated voice or not. Then, I\'ll follow the exact same steps for the vlog, too. And the only thing that will change is which video'

[14:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'ll reference here. &gt;&gt; We\'ve been walking like 20 minutes and every street looks like this. Wait, come here. Look at this. The tiles, the river down there. Yeah, I\'m not leaving this place.'

[14:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And this also gave us pretty similar results to before. And it overall made our videos much higher quality and more realistic. So from here I can generate as many videos as I want and bring every'

[14:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'one of them back to this exact voice without touching our character or her audio again. Now the last thing I need from these videos is the quality. So I\'ll head over to upscale and hit upload media. In here I can upload the videos I'

[14:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'need. And I\'ll choose Proteius as the model and 4K for the quality. And this upscaling tool doesn\'t just fill in pixels but adds genuine details so the videos actually look good and postable.'

[15:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Plus, because we didn\'t generate everything in native 4K, we saved a lot of valuable credits. So, with one character sheet, a few elements, and a custom voice, you can make [music] endless variations of avatar videos, and'

[15:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your character, your locations, and her voice stay the same in every single one. And we didn\'t need a camera, a studio, or to hire a single person to do any of it. The best part is that this whole'

[15:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'workflow is repeatable with any character you want, all under [music] one platform. So, if you want to start making your own realistic AI avatars, click the link in the description and go sign up to Higsfield. Thanks for watching and I\'ll see you in the next'

[15:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'one.'
